<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout>
    <title>Chapter 1 Review</title>

    <p>
        <alert>MTH 124</alert>
    </p>
<page>
    <activity>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                A national park records data regarding the total fox population <m>F</m> over a 12 
                month period, where <m>t=0</m> means January 1, <m>t=1</m> means February 1, and so on. Below is the table of values they recorded:
            </p>
            <tabular>
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell><m>t</m> (month)</cell>
                    <cell>0</cell>
                    <cell>1</cell>
                    <cell>2</cell>
                    <cell>3</cell>
                    <cell>4</cell>
                    <cell>5</cell>
                    <cell>6</cell>
                    <cell>7</cell>
                    <cell>8</cell>
                    <cell>9</cell>
                    <cell>10</cell>
                    <cell>11</cell>
                </row>
                <row >
                    <cell><m>F</m> (foxes)</cell>
                    <cell>150</cell>
                    <cell>143</cell>
                    <cell>125</cell>
                    <cell>100</cell>
                    <cell>75</cell>
                    <cell>57</cell>
                    <cell>50</cell>
                    <cell>57</cell>
                    <cell>75</cell>
                    <cell>100</cell>
                    <cell>125</cell>
                    <cell>143</cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </introduction>
        <task workspace="0cm">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Is <m>t</m> a function of <m>F</m>?  Is <m>F</m> a function of <m>t</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-10-Review-soln">
                <p>
                    The first one says <m>F</m> would be the input, so <m>t</m>
                    is not a function of <m>F</m>, since the number of foxes does not
                    always correspond to one unique month (ex: 143 foxes could be month 1 or 11...)
                    But <m>F</m> is a function of <m>t</m> since one <m>t</m> always corresponds
                    to one unique output of foxes.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task workspace="0.1cm">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let <m>g(t)=F</m> denote the fox population in month <m>t</m>. 
                    Find all solutions(s) to the equation <m>{g(t)=125}</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-10-Review-soln">
                <p>
                    <m>t=2, 10</m>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find <m>AV_{[0,11]}</m> for the function <m>g(t)=F</m>.  What does it mean in context?  Does it tell you
                    everything thing that happened over the year?
                </p>

            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-10-Review-soln">
                <p>
                    <m>AV_{[0,11]}= \frac{g(11)-g(0)}{11-0} = \frac{143-150}{11} = \frac{-7}{11} \approx -0.64</m> foxes per year. 
                    The population of foxes decreased on average by 0.64 foxes per year 
                    during the 12 month period.  This only tells us the average between the 
                    very beginning and very end of the time period, it tells us nothing about what
                    happened in between.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task workspace="0.2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Suppose we have a function <m>h(t) = g(t)+50</m>. What kind of transformation
                    does that apply to <m>g(t)</m>? 
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-10-Review-soln">
                <p>
                    Shifts <m>g(t)</m> up (vertically) by 50.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task workspace="0.2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Consider again <m>h(t) = g(t)+50</m>. What is <m>h(7)</m>?  
                    Is there a way we can think of <m>h(t)</m> in the context of foxes?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-10-Review-soln">
                <p>
                    <m>h(7) = g(7)+50 = 57+50 = 107</m> foxes.  In context, 
                    <m>h(t)</m> adds 50 more foxes to every year of data.  (Maybe 
                    we missed a bunch?)
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task workspace="0.2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Suppose we have a different function <m>k(t) = g(t-1)</m>. What kind of transformation
                    does that apply to <m>g(t)</m>? Can we relate that back to context?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-10-Review-soln">
                <p>
                    Shifts <m>g(t)</m> right by one.  In context, the year is shift one 
                    to the right, as if we got the month wrong by one.  But the number of 
                    foxes are unaffected, just shifted by a month.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task workspace="0.1cm">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Consider again <m>k(t) = g(t-1)</m>. What is <m>k(5)</m>? 
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-10-Review-soln">
                <p>
                    <m>k(5) = g(5-1) = g(4) = 75</m>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task workspace="0.1cm">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Suppose the same national park records data regarding the total 
                    wolf population <m>W</m> over the same 12 month period. Below is the table of values 
                    they recorded:
                </p>
                <tabular>
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell><m>t</m> (month)</cell>
                    <cell>0</cell>
                    <cell>1</cell>
                    <cell>2</cell>
                    <cell>3</cell>
                    <cell>4</cell>
                    <cell>5</cell>
                    <cell>6</cell>
                    <cell>7</cell>
                    <cell>8</cell>
                    <cell>9</cell>
                    <cell>10</cell>
                    <cell>11</cell>
                </row>
                <row >
                    <cell><m>W</m> (wolves)</cell>
                    <cell>110</cell>
                    <cell>101</cell>
                    <cell>100</cell>
                    <cell>92</cell>
                    <cell>60</cell>
                    <cell>50</cell>
                    <cell>32</cell>
                    <cell>40</cell>
                    <cell>50</cell>
                    <cell>60</cell>
                    <cell>91</cell>
                    <cell>101</cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
            <p>
                Let <m>p(t)=W</m> denote the wolf population in month <m>t</m>.  What 
                is <m>p(0)</m>?
            </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-10-Review-soln">
                <p>
                    We just get it from the table: <m>p(0)=110</m>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task workspace="0.1cm">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Taking <m>p(t)</m> to be the number of wolves and <m>g(t)</m> to be the number of 
                    foxes in month <m>t</m>, let's make a new function <m>q(t) = p(t) + g(t)</m> which 
                    measures the total number of foxes and wolves.  What is 
                    <m>q(4)</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-10-Review-soln">
                <p>
                    Plug in 4 to the new function:  <m>q(4) = p(4)+g(4) = 60+75 = 135</m> 
                    foxes and wolves total in the park in month 4.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task workspace="0.1cm">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A completely terrible way to represent the fox population is with 
                    a quadratic function given by the equation <m>{s(t) = 3(t-6)^2+61}</m>.
                    (While it's terrible for many reasons, it's almost the best quadratic function
                    we can get, ask your professor if you're interested in why.). What is 
                    the vertex of this parabola?

                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-10-Review-soln">
                <p>
                    This is already in vertex form so we can pick it directly from the equation:
                    vertex is <m>(6,61)</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task workspace="0.1cm">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Consider still <m>{s(t) = 3(t-6)^2+61}</m>.
                    If this function was all we knew about the fox population and 
                    we didn't know any data, what would this tell us about the fewest number 
                    of foxes we would find in the park?  That is, what is the range of <m>s(t)</m>?

                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-10-Review-soln">
                <p>
                    The parabola opens upwards and has vertex <m>(6,61)</m>. 
                    Since it opens up, the vertex is the lowest point on our 
                    curve, so we will never get below 61 as an output.  That is, 
                    this tells us the fewest foxes would be 61, and the range 
                    is <m>[61,\infty)</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task workspace="0.1cm">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Consider still <m>{s(t) = 3(t-6)^2+61}</m>.  Does <m>s(t)</m> 
                    have an inverse?  If so, find it.  If not, why not.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-10-Review-soln">
                <p>
                    No inverse - it fails the horizontal line test.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task workspace="0.1cm">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What about the original function <m>F=g(t)</m> that described
                    the fox data.  Does that have an inverse?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-10-Review-soln">
                <p>
                     No inverse, because <m>F=125</m> corresponds to <m>t=2, 10</m> for example.
                   
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </activity>

    </page>
    <page>
        <activity>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Find the following.
                </p>
            </introduction>
            <task workspace="0.7in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        The equation of a line that goes through the points 
                        <m>(-14,22)</m> and <m>(100,43)</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            <solution component="S1-10-Review-soln">
                <p>
                    Slope is <m>AV_{[-14,100]}</m> or <m>m = \frac{43 - 22}{100-(-14)} = \frac{21}{114}</m>. 
                    So the line is <md>y=\frac{21}{114}(x-(-14))+22.</md>  It will look 
                    slightly different if you used the other point, or put it in slope-intercept form. 
                </p>
            </solution>
            </task>
            <task workspace="0.7in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        The equation of a line that has slope 6 and goes through
                        the point <m>(2,7)</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            <solution component="S1-10-Review-soln">
                <p>
                    We have everything already: <m>y=6(x-2)+7</m>
                </p>
            </solution>
            </task>
            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        The average rate of change <m>AV_{[1,7]}</m> for the function
                        <m>{f(x)=\frac{7}{x} + 12}</m>.  If <m>f(x)</m> had units of meters 
                        and <m>x</m> had units of seconds, what would be the units of 
                        <m>AV_{[1,7]}</m>?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            <solution component="S1-10-Review-soln">
                <p>
                    <m>AV_{[1,7]}=\frac{f(7)-f(1)}{7-1} = \frac{13-19}{6}=-\frac66 = -1</m>. 
                    Units would be meters per second.
                </p>
            </solution>
            </task>
            <task workspace="1cm">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        If <m>f(x) = 3+2x</m> and <m>g(x) = x^2 -1</m>, find 
                        <m>f(g(0))</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            <solution component="S1-10-Review-soln">
                <p>
                    Since <m>g(0) = -1</m>, then <m>f(g(0)) = f(-1) =1</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
            </task>
            <task workspace="1cm">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        If <m>f(x) = 3+2x</m> and <m>g(x) = x^2 -1</m>, find an equation for
                        <m>f(g(x))</m>.  (No need to simplify.)
                    </p>
                </statement>
            <solution component="S1-10-Review-soln">
                <p>
                    Plug in <m>g(x)</m> into <m>f(x)</m>, so 
                    <md>
                        f(g(x)) = f(x^2 - 1) = 3+2(x^2-1)
                    </md>
                    
                </p>
            </solution>
            </task>
            <task workspace="1cm">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        If <m>y = f(x) = 3+2x</m>, find an equation for <m>f^{-1}(y)</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution component="S1-10-Review-soln">
                    <p>
                        Start with <m>y=3+2x</m> and solve for x:
                        <m>{y-3 = 2x}</m>, then <m>{\frac{y-3}{2} = x}</m>. 
                        So 
                        <md>
                            x = f^{-1}(y) = \frac{y-3}{2}
                        </md>
                        
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>
        </activity>
    </page>
    
</handout>